// The `prelude.mcp` sub-module: MCP in both directions, as ordinary agent values. Outbound, `provide`
// plugs a server's tools into a program for the extent of a scope; inbound, `serve` publishes a
// program's own agents as an MCP server. Both route to the runtime's `mcp` reactor (an in-runtime MCP
// client / server — no FFI sidecar, nothing to install).
//
// A tool is an agent: the runtime mints one agent value per server tool carrying the server-declared
// name / description / input schema, so `reflection.get_metadata` reads it and a call is validated
// against that schema before anything runs. Each minted tool carries its `{url, auth}` descriptor and
// its runtime scope identity; the runtime reconnects lazily per descriptor and closes the connection
// when the last `provide` on it returns.
//
// The scope: `provide` mints a caller-chosen `Scope` marker for its continuation's row and discharges
// it from its own, and a tool carries `Scope` in its row, so a tool is callable exactly inside its
// `provide`. Discharge belongs to `provide` alone, since only it opens the connection. Distinct
// nullary markers never merge, so `katari mcp pull`, which declares a module-local marker per server,
// gives each pulled server its own scope. A call reaching the runtime after its `provide` returned is
// rejected with a typed `server_error`.

@"The MCP server rejected a listing or a tool call, or the transport failed (the server-reported or
transport message). It is also the scope backstop: a tool called after its `provide` returned reports
its closed scope this way. A retried call reconnects."
data server_error(message: string)

@"The server rejected the `headers` key material, so a retry with the same material gives the same
answer and the key the program supplies is what changes. It is not raised on the `oauth` path: an
unusable `oauth` credential pauses the run on an authorization escalation instead of failing it."
data auth_error(message: string)

@"Header-based access: @values@ ride on every request to the server (each value may be a secret,
such as a bearer key). Anonymous access is `headers(values = {})`."
data headers(@"The headers sent on every request, by name; a value may be a secret." values: record[string of private])

@"A named OAuth credential, stored server-side. The runtime injects and refreshes its tokens itself;
the name is not a secret. While the name has no usable credential, any use pauses the run on a
`prelude.oauth.authorize` escalation — answering it establishes the credential and resumes the run."
data oauth(@"The stored credential's registered name; not a secret." name: string)

// How `provide` authenticates with the server: explicit headers, or a named OAuth credential.
type auth = headers | oauth

@"The built-in scope capability for the dynamic `provide` path: a nullary phantom marker, carried in a
tool's effect row, never performable, never handleable. `provide` mints it for its continuation's row
and discharges it from its own, so a tool tagged `scope` cannot outlive the `provide` that introduced
it. A generated `katari mcp pull` binding declares its own module-local marker instead."
effect scope

// One server tool, as an ordinary agent value, gated by the `Scope` marker `provide` mints. (Type
// synonyms take no docs.)
type tool[effect Scope] = agent never -> unknown with io | Scope | prelude.throw[server_error | auth_error]

// A server's tools, keyed by tool name — call one by key, or hand the values (the
// flat array of tools) to an AI loop wholesale. `Scope` is the marker the tools are gated by.
type toolbox[effect Scope] = record[tool[Scope]]

@"Plug an MCP server's tools into a program for the extent of @continuation@ — the `use mcp.provide(url = ..., auth = ...)` form. The runtime lists the server, hands @continuation@ a `toolbox[Scope]` of one runtime-minted agent per tool, and settles with the continuation's result. `Scope` rides the continuation's row and is discharged from `provide`'s own, so a tool is callable exactly inside the block; pass the marker explicitly as the first `[...]` argument (`mcp.provide[mcp.scope](...)`), and the toolbox binder's annotation agrees with it.

A missing or dead `oauth` credential pauses on an authorization escalation and retries once answered; rejected `headers` material throws `auth_error`, and a rejected listing throws `server_error`. A tool called after the scope closes is rejected as a typed `server_error`.

Two connections sharing one marker share a scope, so their tools become mutually substitutable in a row carrying it — a type-level distinction only, since routing follows each tool value's own `{url, auth}` descriptor. A marker per connection keeps them apart, which a `katari mcp pull` binding does automatically. When the toolbox is never read, `open` is the listing-free form of this same contract."
external agent provide[effect Scope, R, effect E](
  @"The MCP server's endpoint URL (streamable HTTP, with SSE fallback)." url: string,
  @"How the runtime authenticates with the server: `headers(...)` or `oauth(...)`." auth: auth,
  @"The scope body (bound by `use`): receives the toolbox, and the tools are callable exactly while it runs." continuation: agent (value: toolbox[Scope]) -> R with E | Scope,
  @"A model-facing name prefix for this server's tools: with `prefix = \"notion\"` a server tool `search` is published as `notion_search` (the toolbox key and the tool's metadata name — the wire still carries the server-declared name). Set it when two connected servers expose the same tool name; `null` keeps the server's own names." prefix: string | null ?= null,
) -> R with E | io from "mcp"

@"Open an MCP server connection for the extent of @continuation@ without listing the server — the
listing-free form of `provide`, same scoped contract, no toolbox. @continuation@ starts immediately
with `null`, no server round-trip happens at open, and the connection tears down when it settles.
Tools are reached statically through `mcp.call[Scope, T]` under the same `{url, auth}` descriptor — the
shape a `katari mcp pull` binding generates — so prefer `open` wherever the toolbox would be ignored.
Because nothing is contacted at open, auth surfaces at the first `mcp.call`: a missing or dead `oauth`
credential pauses that call on an authorization escalation, and rejected `headers` material or server
failures are its typed throws."
external agent open[effect Scope, R, effect E](
  @"The MCP server's endpoint URL (streamable HTTP, with SSE fallback)." url: string,
  @"How the runtime authenticates with the server: `headers(...)` or `oauth(...)`." auth: auth,
  @"The scope body (bound by `use`): receives `null` (no toolbox is minted), and `mcp.call[Scope, ...]` is callable exactly while it runs." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with E | Scope,
) -> R with E | io from "mcp"

@"One connection of a `provide_all` set: the server, its auth, and an optional model-facing name
prefix (see `provide`'s @prefix@ — set it on the colliding connections when two servers expose the
same tool name)."
data connection(
  @"The MCP server's endpoint URL." url: string,
  @"How the runtime authenticates with the server: `headers(...)` or `oauth(...)`." auth: auth,
  @"The model-facing tool-name prefix, or `null` for the server's own names." prefix: string | null ?= null,
)

@"Open a set of MCP connections under one scope for the extent of @continuation@ — the
data-driven form of `provide`. The marker is minted once whatever the array holds, so an optional
connection is an `if` in the data rather than a branch around the `use`. The continuation receives one
toolbox per connection, in input order. Connections open one by one in order and tear down in reverse;
any connection's listing failure is the same typed error a lone `provide` raises, and tears the
already-open ones down. An empty array runs @continuation@ with no toolboxes, in the same row shape, so
callers do not branch on emptiness. It is an external rather than a composition over `provide` because
only the runtime opening the scope can discharge a never-minted marker."
external agent provide_all[effect Scope, R, effect E](
  @"The connections to open, in order; empty is fine." connections: array[connection],
  @"The scope body (bound by `use`): receives one toolbox per connection, in input order." continuation: agent (value: array[toolbox[Scope]]) -> R with E | Scope,
) -> R with E | io from "mcp"

@"Call one tool on an MCP server directly — the static counterpart of `provide`, with no listing and no minted agent value, and the seam a `katari mcp pull` binding is built on. @arguments@ is a plain value tree (a `file` in it becomes base64 in place, the `http.json` slot contract), validated by the server alone, so a rejected call throws `server_error`.

The result is read as a value and checked against @T@ inside the runtime: a `$katari_ref` handle object becomes a real `file`, a plain-text reply reads into a string-shaped @T@, and a mismatch throws `validation_error`. Instantiate @T@ with `unknown` to keep the reply raw — the wire-to-value conversion is unconditional either way, never schema-directed. Both @T@ and the phantom @Scope@ are uninferable, so write both: `mcp.call[Scope, T](url = ..., ...)`.

Scope-gated the same way as a minted tool: the runtime rejects a call with no live scope for that descriptor as a typed `server_error`."
external agent call[effect Scope, T](
  @"The MCP server's endpoint URL (the same descriptor the surrounding `provide` scope was opened with)." url: string,
  @"How the runtime authenticates with the server: `headers(...)` or `oauth(...)`." auth: auth,
  @"The server-declared tool name." tool: string,
  @"The tool's arguments object, as a plain value tree; only the server validates it." arguments: unknown,
) -> T with Scope | io | prelude.throw[server_error | auth_error | json.validation_error] from "mcp"

@"Publish @tools@ as a live MCP server for the extent of @subscriber@. The runtime mints a fresh,
unguessable capability URL and serves the record's agents there — each record key is the published tool
name, each agent's declared signature the tool's advertised schema. The URL is the key: possession
grants access, and nothing else does. @subscriber@ receives the URL, hands it to whoever should
connect, and stays alive while calls should be served; when it returns (or the run is cancelled) the
URL deactivates and its result becomes `serve`'s result.

A served agent is self-contained: its effects are handled, or are at most `io` and this module's
throws. A served tool that throws or panics with no handler of its own cancels the whole endpoint, its
failure proxying up and failing the run unless caught above @subscriber@; a handler around a tool's
body turns that into a per-request outcome."
external agent serve[R, effect E](
  @"The agents to publish, keyed by the tool name each is published under." tools: toolbox[scope],
  @"The endpoint's owner: receives the capability URL, and the server serves exactly while it runs." subscriber: agent (url: string) -> R with E,
) -> R with E | io from "mcp"
